    77	In this paper we use the nonrepresentable ring $E_{p}^{(m)}$ to introduce public key cryptosystems in noncommutative settings and based on the Semigroup Action Problem and the Decomposition Problem respectively.
    78	\end{abstract}
    79	
    80	
    81	\section{Introduction}
    82	\label{Intro}
    83	
    84	
    85	Most public-key cryptosystems are based on certain specific problems of number theory. 
    86	One of these problems is the \emph{Integer Factorization Problem} (IFP) over the ring $\mathbb{Z}_{n}$, being $n$ the product of two large prime numbers; the well known cryptosystem RSA \cite{Rivest1978} is based in this problem. 
    87	The second classical problem is the \emph{Discrete Logarithm Problem} (DLP) over a finite field $\mathbb{Z}_{p}$, being $p$ a large prime; the Diffie-Hellman key exchange protocol \cite{Diffie1976} and ElGamal protocol \cite{ElGamal1985} are based on this problem. 
    88	In general, we can say that their robustness depends on the computational difficulty of solving certain mathematical problems over finite commutative algebraic structures. 
    89	Some efficient attacks based on the commutative property of these structures are well known: Quadratic Sieve, General Number Field Sieve, Pollard's rho algorithm, Index-Calculus, etc.\ (see, for example, \cite{Menezes1996bk,Stinson1995bk}, and the references within these books).
    90	
    91	
    92	This fact, together with the increase of the computational power of modern computers, has made these techniques become more and more insecure.
    93	As a result there exists an active field of research known as noncommutative algebraic cryptography (see, for example, \cite{Anshel1999,Ko2007,Ko2000,Sakalauskas2003,Sidelnikov1994}) aiming to develop and analyse new cryptosystems and key exchange protocols based on noncommutative cryptographic platforms.
    94	A very good exposition of the problems underlying in this noncommutative approach can be found in \cite{Myasnikov2008bk} and some of them are the following, where $G$ is a nonabelian group:
    95	
    96	\begin{itemize}
    97	\item \emph{Conjugator Search Problem (CSP):}
    98	Given $(x,y) \in G \times G$, the problem is to find $z \in G$ such that $y = z^{-1} x z$.
    99	\item \emph{Decomposition Problem (DP):}
   100	Given $(x,y) \in G \times G$ and $S \subseteq G$, the problem is to find $z_{1}, z_{2} \in S$ such that $y = z_{1} x z_{2}$.
   101	\item \emph{Symmetrical Decomposition Problem (SDP):}
   102	Given $(x,y) \in G \times G$ and $m,n \in \mathbb{Z}$, the problem is to find $z \in G$ such that $y = z^{m} x z^{n}$.
   103	\item \emph{Generalized Symmetrical Decomposition Problem (GSDP):}
   104	Given $(x,y) \in G \times G$, $S \subseteq G$ and $m, n \in \mathbb{Z}$, the problem is to find $z \in S$ such that $y = z^{m} x z^{n}$.
   105	\end{itemize}
   106	
   107	
   108	Note that for the DP we can assume that $G$ is a semigroup, since in this case we do not need invertible elements.
   109	
   110	
   111	Several authors have proposed and used certain nonabelian groups for
   112	key exchange problems. In
   113	\cite{Anshel2001,Anshel1999,Ko2007,Ko2000}, the authors suggest to
   114	use braid groups as platform groups for their respective protocols.
   115	In \cite{Paeng2001}, the authors propose a public key cryptosystem
   116	whose security is based on the DLP for the automorphism defined by
   117	the conjugation operation and the difficulty to find the conjugate
   118	element on finite nonabelian groups. In \cite{Shpilrain2006}, the
   119	authors suggest the use of a finite representation of a nonabelian
   120	group, called Thomson's group, to develop a public key cryptosystem,
   121	where they raised for the first time the difficulty to find a
   122	solution for the SDP. Finally, in \cite{Thomas2008}, the authors
   123	propose a cryptosystem whose robustness is based on the difficulty
   124	to solve the CSP and SDP over any noncommutative algebraic
   125	structure.
   126	
   127	
   128	In the noncommutative setting, we can find different implementations based on the Diffie-Hellman protocol in matrix rings, for different kind of matrices \cite{Climent2006b,Stickel2005,Yoo2000}.
   129	A detachable recent work in this setting is \cite{Mahalanobis2013} where the author shows the usability of a certain class of matrices that arise a noncommutative framework to define the ElGamal cryptosystem and the corresponding Diffie-Hellman protocol as well as its eficiency.
   130	Another system based on the discrete logarithm problem in the authomorphisms group is the so-called MOR cryptosystem, recently described for authomorphisms of $p$-groups whose order is coprime to $p$ in \cite{Mahalanobis2008b,Mahalanobis2015}.
   131	
   132	
   133	Moreover, the idea to develop systems of open distribution keys as well as session key exchange protocols, on the basis of noncommutative (semi)groups, is present in \cite{Mahalanobis2008,Sakalauskas2003,Sidelnikov1994}.
   134	
   135	
   136	Finally, with the idea to generalize the protocols based on groups
   137	and take advantage of the difficulty of to solve the DLP in these
   138	groups, Shpilrain and Zapata in \cite{Shpilrain2006b} give a general
   139	framework using actions of groups to define key exchange protocols.
   140	In this setting, Maze \emph{et al.} \cite{Maze2007} introduce the
   141	\emph{Semigroup Action Problem} (SAP) as:
   142	\begin{quote}
   143	Let $G$ be a finite semigroup acting on a finite set $S$.
   144	Given $x, y \in S$ with $y = g \cdot x$ for some $g \in G$, find $h \in G$ such that $y = h \cdot x$.
   145	\end{quote}
   146	In this way, any Abelian semigroup $G$ acting on a finite set $S$ provides a Diffie-Hellman key exchange protocol and an ElGamal protocol (cf. \cite{Maze2007}).
   147	
   148	\medskip
   149	
   150	Our aim in this paper is to provide public key cryptosystems in a noncommutative setting given by the ring $E_p^{(m)}$ based on the SAP and DP. 
   151	The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. 
   152	In Section~\ref{ringEpm} we remind some properties of the nonrepresentable ring $E_p^{(m)}$ that will be used through this paper. 
   153	In Section~\ref{PKCSAP} we use an action of the ring $E_p^{(m)}$ to introduce a public key cryptosystem based on the SAP. 
   154	Finally, in Section~\ref{PKCDP} we use this ring to give a public key cryptosystem based on the DP and we relate security of both introduced cryptosystems.
   155	
   156	
   157	\section{The ring $E_{p}^{(m)}$}
   158	\label{ringEpm}
   159	
   160	
   161	Bergman \cite{Bergman1974} proved that $\mathrm{End}(\mathbb{Z}_{p} \times \mathbb{Z}_{p^{2}})$ is a semilocal ring that cannot be embedded in matrices over any commutative ring.
   162	In \cite{Climent2011a} the authors showed that $\mathrm{End}(\mathbb{Z}_{p} \times \mathbb{Z}_{p^{2}})$
   163	is isomorphic to a ring whose elements can be expressed as square matrices whose rows are given by elements in $\mathbb{Z}_{p}$ and $\mathbb{Z}_{p^{2}}$ respectively and with an arithmetic that is similar to matrix addition and multiplication.
   164	Then they use this ring to define a key exchange protocol (see also \cite{Climent2011c,Climent2012c}).
   165	However this was cryptoanalyzed in \cite{Kamal2012} using some invertible elements, which motivated the introduction of an extension of such a ring \cite{Climent2014d} such that almost all elements are noninvertible
   166	
   167	
   168	This new ring is defined as the set (see \cite[Theorem~1]{Climent2014d})
   169	\[
   170	  E_{p}^{(m)}
   171	  =
   172	  \left\{
   173	    [a_{ij}] \in \mathrm{Mat}_{m \times m} (\mathbb{Z})
   174	    \ | \
   175	    a_{ij} \in \mathbb{Z}_{p^{i}} \ \text{if} \ i \leq j,
   176	    \ \text{and} \
   177	    a_{ij} \in p^{i-j} \mathbb{Z}_{p^{j}} \ \text{if} \ i > j
   178	  \right\}
   179	\]
   180	with the addition and the multiplication defined, respectively, as follows
   181	\begin{gather*}
   182	  \big[
   183	    a_{ij}
   184	  \big]
   185	  +
   186	  \big[
   187	    b_{ij}
   188	  \big]
   189	  =
   190	  \big[
   191	    (a_{ij}+b_{ij}) \bmod{p^{i}}
   192	  \big], \\
   193	  \big[
   194	    a_{ij}
   195	  \big]
   196	  \cdot
   197	  \big[
   198	    b_{ij}
   199	  \big]
   200	  =
   201	  \left[
   202	    \left(
   203	      \sum_{k=1}^{m} a_{ik} b_{kj}
   204	    \right)
   205	    \bmod{p^{i}}
   206	  \right].
   207	\end{gather*}
   208	Here $\mathrm{Mat}_{m \times m} (\mathbb{Z})$ denotes the set of $m \times m$ matrices with entries in $\mathbb{Z}$, and $p^{r} \mathbb{Z}_{p^{s}}$ denotes the set $\left\{p^{r} u \ | \ u \in \mathbb{Z}_{p^{s}} \right\}$ for positive integers $r$ y $s$.
   209	Moreover, $\left| E_{p}^{(m)} \right| = p^{(2 m^{3} + 3 m^{2} + m)/6}$.
   210	
   211	
   212	The following results on the ring $E_{p}^{(m)}$ can be found in \cite{Climent2014d}.
   213	
   214	
   215	\begin{thm}[Theorem~3 of \cite{Climent2014d}]
   216	Let $A = [a_{ij}] \in E_{p}^{(m)}$.
   217	Then $A$ is invertible if and only if $a_{ii} \not\equiv_{p} 0$ for every $i=1,2,\dots,m$.
   218	\end{thm}
   219	
   220	
   221	As a consequence, the number of invertible elements in $E_{p}^{(m)}$ is $p^{(2 m^{3} + 3 m^{2} - 5 m)/6}$ (see \cite[Corollary~1]{Climent2014d}).
   222	Moreover, the fraction of invertible elements in $E_{p}^{(m)}$ is
   223	\begin{math}
   224	  \left(
   225	    \frac{p-1}{p}
   226	  \right)^{m}.
   227	\end{math}
   228	So, an adequate election of $p$ and $m$ may result that the number of invertible elements in $E_{p}^{(m)}$ is almost zero (see \cite[Tables~1 and 2]{Climent2014d}).
   229	
   230	
   231	Another needed property is the characterization of the center of the
   232	ring $E_{p}^{(m)}$. The following result provides such a
   233	characterization.
   234	
   235	\begin{thm}[Page~359 of \cite{Climent2014d}] \label{centerEpm}
   236	The center of $E_{p}^{(m)}$ is given by the set
   237	\[
   238	  Z(E_{p}^{(m)})
   239	  =
   240	  \left\{
   241	    [a_{ij}] \in E_{p}^{(m)}
   242	    \ | \
   243	    a_{ii} = \sum_{j=0}^{i-1} p^{j} u_{j}, \ \text{with} \ u_{j} \in \mathbb{Z}_{p}
   244	    \ \text{and} \
   245	    a_{ij} = 0 \ \text{if} \ i \neq j
   246	  \right\}.
   247	\]
   248	As a consequence the number of central elements in $E_{p}^{(m)}$ is $p^{m}$.
   249	\end{thm}
   250	
   251	
   252	
   253	
   254	\section{A public key cryptosystem based on the SAP}
   255	\label{PKCSAP}
   256	
   257	
   258	Our aim in this section is to use the arithmetic of the ring
   259	$E_{p}^{(m)}$ and an action of this ring to define a public key
   260	cryptosystem in a noncommutative setting.
   261	
   262	
   263	Thus, based on the multiplication defined on the ring $E_{p}^{(m)}$ and the structure of its elements we may define an action of the ring $E_{p}^{(m)}$ over the set $\mathbb{Z}_{p} \times \mathbb{Z}_{p^{2}} \times \cdots \times \mathbb{Z}_{p^{m}}$.
   264	As we will show, this action will arise a public key cryptosystem based on the SAP (cf.\ \cite{Maze2007}).
   265	
   266	
   267	Let us consider the center $Z(E_{p}^{(m)})$ of the ring $E_{p}^{(m)}$.
   268	For a given $M \in E_{p}^{(m)}$, let $\mathrm{Cen}(M)$ be the set of elements $X$ in $E_{p}^{(m)}$ such that $X M = M X$.
   269	The algorithm is given by the following
   270	
   271	
   272	\begin{alg} \label{sap}
   273	Let $M \in E_{p}^{(m)}$ be a public value and $S \in \mathbb{Z}_{p} \times \mathbb{Z}_{p^{2}} \times \cdots \times \mathbb{Z}_{p^{m}}$ the message that Bob wants to send Alice.
   274	Then:
   275	\begin{enumerate}
   276	\item \label{sap.1}
   277	Alice chooses $R \in \mathbb{Z}_{p} \times \mathbb{Z}_{p^{2}} \times \cdots \times \mathbb{Z}_{p^{m}}$, $F \in \mathrm{Cen}(M)$ and computes $T = F \cdot R$.
   278	
   279	\item \label{sap.2}
   280	Alice makes public the pair $(R,T)$, keeping secret her private key $F$.
   281	
   282	\item \label{sap.3}
   283	Bob chooses randomly $G = \displaystyle \sum_{i=0}^{k} C_{i} M^{i}$, where $C_{i} \in Z(E_{p}^{(m)})$ and sends Alice the pair
   284	\begin{math}
   285	  (H, D)
   286	  =
   287	  (G \cdot R, S + G \cdot T).
   288	\end{math}
   289	
   290	\item \label{sap.4}
   291	Alice gets the secret by computing $S = D - F \cdot H$.
   292	\end{enumerate}
   293	\end{alg}
   294	
   295	
   296	Note that the commutativity of $F$ and $G$ gives the correctness of the preceding algorithm and that Alice and Bob could exchange the way they choose the elements appearing through it.
   297	
   298	
   299	As it is asserted in the general case in \cite{Maze2007} we can observe that breaking the preceding algorithm involves solving the SAP, i.e., given the values $R$ and $T = F \cdot R \in \mathbb{Z}_{p} \times \mathbb{Z}_{p^{2}} \times \cdots \times \mathbb{Z}_{p^{m}}$, find $A \in E_{p}^{(m)}$ such that $T = A \cdot
   300	R$.
   301	
   302	
   303	To understand the size of the underlying SAP, let $p$ be a prime, consider $x \in \mathbb{Z}_{p^{m}}$ coprime to $p$, and let $n$ be the order of $x$.
   304	Now let $M = [a_{ij}]$ be the element of $E_{p}^{(m)}$ given by
   305	\[
   306	  a_{ij}
   307	  =
   308	  \begin{cases}
   309	    x, & \text{for $i=j=m$}, \\
   310	    0, & \text{otherwise}.
   311	  \end{cases}
   312	\]
   313	For $k=1,2,\ldots$, assume that $M^{k} = [a_{ij}^{(k)}]$; then
   314	\[
   315	  a_{ij}^{(k)}
   316	  =
   317	  \begin{cases}
   318	    x^{k}, & \text{for $i=j=m$}, \\
   319	    0,     & \text{otherwise}.
   320	  \end{cases}

exec
/bin/bash -lc "zcat 1606.05457.tar | nl -ba | sed -n '320,680p'" in /home/gson/gvrepo/results/1606.05457
 succeeded in 0ms:
   320	  \end{cases}
   321	\]
   322	Thus it is clear that $M^{n+1} = M$ and $M, M^{2}, \ldots, M^{n}$ are pairwise different.
   323	Since $M^{0} = I$ (by definition), the elements of $G = \displaystyle \sum_{k=0}^{n} C_{k} M^{k}$ are in
   324	$\mathrm{Cen}(M)$ and have the form
   325	\begin{equation} \label{eqG}
   326	  G
   327	  =
   328	  \mathrm{diag}
   329	  \left(
   330	    c_{0}^{(0)},
   331	    c_{0}^{(0)} + c_{1}^{(0)} p,
   332	    \ldots,
   333	    \sum_{l=0}^{m-2} c_{l}^{(0)} p^{l},
   334	    \sum_{l=0}^{m-1} c_{l}^{(0)} p^{l}
   335	    +
   336	    \sum_{k=1}^{n}
   337	    \left(
   338	      \sum_{l=0}^{m-1} c_{l}^{(k)} p^{l}
   339	    \right)
   340	    x^{k}
   341	  \right)
   342	\end{equation}
   343	where, by Theorem~\ref{centerEpm},
   344	\[
   345	  C_{k}
   346	  =
   347	  \mathrm{diag}
   348	  \left(
   349	    c_{0}^{(k)},
   350	    c_{0}^{(k)} + c_{1}^{(k)} p,
   351	    \ldots,
   352	    \sum_{l=0}^{m-2} c_{l}^{(k)} p^{l},
   353	    \sum_{l=0}^{m-1} c_{l}^{(k)} p^{l}
   354	  \right).
   355	\]
   356	
   357	
   358	Thus, accordingly to expression (\ref{eqG}), for every possible choice of the element
   359	\[
   360	  C_{0}
   361	  =
   362	  \mathrm{diag}
   363	  \left(
   364	    c_{0}^{(0)},
   365	    c_{0}^{(0)} + c_{1}^{(0)} p,
   366	    \ldots,
   367	    \sum_{l=0}^{m-2} c_{l}^{(0)} p^{l},
   368	    \sum_{l=0}^{m-1} c_{l}^{(0)} p^{l}
   369	  \right)
   370	\]
   371	in $Z(E_{p}^{(m)})$, there are as many elements $G$ as the number of elements of the set
   372	\begin{equation} \label{eq0}
   373	  \left\{
   374	    \sum_{l=0}^{m-1} c_{l}^{(0)} p^{l}
   375	    +
   376	    \sum_{k=1}^{n}
   377	    \left(
   378	      \sum_{l=0}^{m-1} c_{l}^{(k)} p^{l}
   379	    \right)
   380	    x^{k}
   381	    \ | \
   382	    C_{1}, \ldots, C_{n} \in Z(E_{p}^{(m)})
   383	  \right\}.
   384	\end{equation}
   385	
   386	We point out that in the preceding expression, $C_{1}, \ldots, C_{n}$ are not necessarily distinct and many of them could be the zero element in $E_p^{(m)}$.
   387	
   388	But the set given in expression (\ref{eq0})
   389	%\[
   390	%  \left\{
   391	%    \sum_{l=0}^{m-1} c_{l}^{(0)} p^{l}
   392	%    +
   393	%    \sum_{k=1}^{n}
   394	%    \left(
   395	%      \sum_{l=0}^{m-1} c_{l}^{(k)} p^{l}
   396	%    \right)
   397	%    x^{k}
   398	%    \ | \
   399	%    C_{1}, \ldots, C_{n} \in Z(E_{p}^{(m)})
   400	%  \right\}
   401	%\]
   402	may be expressed as
   403	\[
   404	  \left\{
   405	    \sum_{l=0}^{m-1} c_{l}^{(0)} p^{l}
   406	    +
   407	    \sum_{l=0}^{m-1}
   408	    \left(
   409	     \sum_{k=1}^{n} c_{l}^{(k)} x^{k}
   410	    \right)
   411	    p^{l}
   412	    \ | \
   413	    C_{1}, \ldots, C_{n} \in Z(E_{p}^{(m)})
   414	  \right\}
   415	\]
   416	which gives all the elements of $\mathbb{Z}_{p^{m}}$ given that $C_{1}, \ldots ,C_{n}$ may be chosen in such a way that we get the $p$-adic decomposition of every element in $\mathbb{Z}_{p^{m}}$.
   417	Therefore, for every element in $Z(E_{p}^{(m)})$, there are as many elements $G$ as elements in $\mathbb{Z}_{p^{m}}$, which results that we have $p^{m} \cdot p^{m} = p^{2m}$ different possibilities to select the element $G$.
   418	
   419	
   420	An analogous result is obtained for $M = [a_{ij}]$ with
   421	\[
   422	  a_{ij}
   423	  =
   424	  \begin{cases}
   425	    x,         & \text{for $i=j=m$}, \\
   426	    y p^{m-1}, & \text{for $i=m$ and $j=1$}, \\
   427	    0, & \text{otherwise}.
   428	  \end{cases}
   429	\]
   430	and $y \in \mathbb{Z}_{p}$ is an element of order $p-1$.
   431	
   432	
   433	On the other hand, in \cite{Maze2007} the authors show that the nearest the semigroup acting on the corresponding set is to be a group, i.e.\ the bigger the subgroup of units in the semigroup is, the easier is to develop a Polling-Hellman algorithm in the semigroup to compute the corresponding discrete logarithm.
   434	In this case, as it was noted previously, the set of noninvertible elements in $E_{p}^{(m)}$ might be very close to the whole semigroup with an adequate election of $p$ and $m$.
   435	
   436	
   437	An additional property of the preceding algorithm is the use of a different $G$ for every encryption.
   438	In ElGamal cryptosystem \cite{ElGamal1985} the election of a random parameter in every encryption avoids an attack based on a pair plain text-encrypted text, given the existence of invertible elements.
   439	The same idea cannot be developed in this case due to the almost nonexistence of invertible elements.
   440	As in ElGamal case, the election of a different $G$ in every encryption avoids repetition attacks.
   441	
   442	
   443	Now let $(R, T)$ be a public key, with $T = F \cdot R$, where $F \in \mathrm{Cen}(M)$, and let $A$ be a solution of the SAP, i.e., $A \cdot R = T$.
   444	Suppose also that $G$ in step~\ref{sap.3} of Algorithm~\ref{sap} is such that $A G = G A$.
   445	Then, given the encrypted message
   446	\[
   447	  (H, D)
   448	  =
   449	  (G \cdot R, S + G \cdot T)
   450	\]
   451	we have that
   452	\[
   453	  S + G \cdot T - (A G) \cdot R
   454	  =
   455	  S + (G A) \cdot R - (A G) \cdot R
   456	  =
   457	  S.
   458	\]
   459	
   460	
   461	Thus it is crucial that the element $G$ in step~\ref{sap.3} of Algorithm~\ref{sap} does not commute with the solution $A$ of the SAP.
   462	Therefore to avoid that $G$ commute with any such solution, we must require that $G$ is not in the center of the multiplicative semigroup of $E_{p}^{(m)}$, which can be easily checked by requiring that there exists at least a nonzero element out of its main diagonal (see Theorem~\ref{centerEpm}).
   463	
   464	
   465	Then suppose that an attacker tries to find $A \in Z(E_{p}^{(m)})$ being a solution of the SAP, i.e., $A \cdot R = T$.
   466	Thus, again, according to Theorem~\ref{centerEpm}, $A$ must be of the form
   467	\begin{equation} \label{eq3}
   468	  A
   469	  =
   470	%  \mathrm{diag}
   471	%  \left(
   472	%    a_{0},
   473	%    a_{0} + p a_{1},
   474	%    \ldots,
   475	%    a_{0} + p a_{1} + \cdots + p^{m-1} a_{m-1}
   476	%  \right),
   477	  \mathrm{diag}
   478	  \left(
   479	    a_{0},
   480	    a_{0} + a_{1} p,
   481	    \ldots,
   482	    \sum_{j=0}^{m-2} a_{j} p^{j},
   483	    \sum_{j=0}^{m-1} a_{j} p^{j}
   484	  \right),
   485	\end{equation}
   486	with $a_{j} \in \mathbb{Z}_{p}$ for every $j = 0,1,2,\ldots,m-1$.
   487	
   488	
   489	\begin{thm}
   490	Assume that
   491	\[
   492	  R = (r_{0},r_{1},\ldots,r_{m-1})
   493	  \quad \text{and} \quad
   494	  T = (t_{0},t_{1},\ldots,t_{m-1})
   495	\]
   496	are elements in $\mathbb{Z}_{p} \times \mathbb{Z}_{p^{2}} \times \cdots \times \mathbb{Z}_{p^{m}}$ such that $A \cdot R = T$.
   497	If $r_{j} \not\equiv_{p} 0$ for $j=0,1,2,\ldots,m-1$ then
   498	\[
   499	  r_{0}^{-1} t_{0}
   500	  \equiv_{p}
   501	  r_{1}^{-1} t_{1}
   502	  \equiv_{p}
   503	  \cdots
   504	  \equiv_{p}
   505	  r_{m-1}^{-1} t_{m-1}.
   506	\]
   507	\end{thm}
   508	\begin{proof}
   509	Since $A \cdot R = T$ it follows that
   510	\begin{gather}
   511	  a_{0} r_{0} \equiv_{p} t_{0},  \label{eq4} \\
   512	  (a_{0} + p a_{1} + \cdots + p^{j} a_{j}) r_{j}
   513	  \equiv_{p^{j+1}}
   514	  t_{j},
   515	  \quad
   516	  \text{for $j=1,2,\ldots,m-1$}. \label{eq5}
   517	\end{gather}
   518	
   519	Now, from expression (\ref{eq4}) and the fact that $r_{0} \not\equiv_{p} 0$, we obtain that $a_{0} \equiv_{p} r_{0}^{-1} t_{0}$.
   520	
   521	Assume now that $j=1,2,\ldots,m-1$.
   522	From expression (\ref{eq5}) we have that
   523	\[
   524	  (a_{0} + p a_{1} + \cdots + p^{j} a_{j}) r_{j} - t_{j}
   525	  =
   526	  p^{j+1} h,
   527	  \quad \text{for some $h \in \mathbb{Z}$},
   528	\]
   529	so, $p \mid (t_{j} - a_{0} r_{j})$.
   530	Therefore, $a_{0} r_{j} \equiv_{p} t_{j}$ and using the fact that $r_{j} \not\equiv_{p} 0$, we obtain that $a_{0} \equiv_{p} r_{j}^{-1} t_{j}$.
   531	\end{proof}
   532	
   533	
   534	As an immediate consequence we get the following result.
   535	
   536	
   537	\begin{cor} \label{conditionSAP}
   538	Let $F \in E_{p}^{(m)}$ as in Algorithm~\ref{sap} defining a public key $(R,T)$, of some user, with $T = F \cdot R$, being $R = (r_{0},r_{1},\ldots,r_{m-1})$ and $T = (t_{0},t_{1},\ldots,t_{m-1})$ with $r_{j}, t_{j} \in \mathbb{Z}_{p^{j+1}}$, for $j = 0,1,2,\ldots,m-1$.
   539	If $p \nmid r_{j}$ for every $j=0,1,2,\ldots,m-1$ and there exits $k$ such that $r_{k}^{-1} t_{k} \not \equiv_{p} r_{0}^{-1} t_{0}$, then an attacker cannot compute $A \in Z(E_{p}^{(m)})$ such that $T = A \cdot R$.
   540	\end{cor}
   541	
   542	
   543	
   544	\section{A public key cryptosystem based on the DP}
   545	\label{PKCDP}
   546	
   547	
   548	Our aim in this section is to give a trap-door function based on the DP in $E_{p}^{(m)}$ following ElGamal's ideas in the case of the DLP.
   549	ElGamal \cite{ElGamal1985} introduced his cryptosystem based on the Diffie-Hellman key exchange
   550	protocol \cite{Diffie1976}.
   551	Thus we provide a key exchange in the ring $E_{p}^{(m)}$ following the CAKE concept introduced in
   552	\cite[Definition~3]{Shpilrain2006b}.
   553	
   554	To do so, given $M \in E_{p}^{(m)}$, we will consider the set
   555	\begin{equation} \label{eq6}
   556	  H(M)
   557	  =
   558	  \left\{
   559	    \sum_{i=0}^{k} C_{i} M^{i}
   560	    \ | \
   561	    C_{i} \in Z(E_{p}^{(m)}), k \in \mathbb{Z}^{+}
   562	  \right\}
   563	\end{equation}
   564	where $Z(E_{p}^{(m)})$ is the center of the semiring $E_{p}^{(m)}$ (see Theorem~\ref{centerEpm}) and $\mathbb{Z}^{+}$ denotes the set of positive integers.
   565	
   566	
   567	\begin{prot}[DHDP protocol] \label{prot1}
   568	Alice and Bob agree on two public elements $X,M \in E_{p}^{(m)}$ such that $M \notin \mathrm{Cen}(X)$.
   569	\begin{enumerate}
   570	\item Alice chooses two different elements $A_{1}, A_{2} \in H(M)$ and transmits $G_{A} = A_{1} X A_{2}$ to Bob.
   571	\item Bob chooses $B_{1}, B_{2} \in \mathrm{Cen}(M)$ such that $B_{1} X \neq X B_{2}$ and transmits $G_{B} = B_{1} X B_{2}$ to Alice.
   572	\item Alice computes $A_{1} G_{B} A_{2}$.
   573	\item Bob computes $B_{1} G_{A} B_{2}$.
   574	\end{enumerate}
   575	\end{prot}
   576	
   577	
   578	Now it is clear that both Alice and Bob share a common value and that they could exchange their roles in the way they choose their corresponding private information.
   579	The latter is a particular case of \cite[Example 3]{Shpilrain2006b}, where the authors illustrate a protocol based on the DP in a general semigroup.
   580	
   581	
   582	Trying to break the preceding protocol, in its general setting as it is shown in \cite[Example 3]{Shpilrain2006b} gives rise to the following problem directly related to the DP.
   583	
   584	
   585	\begin{defn}
   586	Let $G$ be a semigroup, $A, B \subseteq G$ two subsemigroups such that $a b = b a$ for every $a \in A$ and $b \in B$ and assume that $x \in G$.
   587	The \textbf{DH Decomposition Problem (DHDP)} consists in given two elements $a_{1} x a_{2}$ and $b_{1} x b_{2}$, with $a_{1}, a_{2} \in A$ and $b_{1}, b_{2} \in B$ such that provide a DHDP key exchange as above, find the element $a_{1} b_{1} x b_{2} a_{2}$.
   588	\end{defn}
   589	
   590	
   591	It is immediate that being able to solve the DP implies that we will be able to solve the DHDP.
   592	
   593	
   594	On the other hand, in \cite{Climent2011a} (see also \cite{Climent2011c,Climent2012c}) the authors introduce a key exchange protocol over the noncommutative ring $\mathrm{End}(\mathbb{Z}_{p} \times \mathbb{Z}_{p^{2}})$ that fits with the previous protocol.
   595	In \cite{Kamal2012} the authors solve the DHDP problem using some invertible elements in $\mathrm{End}(\mathbb{Z}_{p} \times \mathbb{Z}_{p^{2}})$ resulting a cryptanalysis of that proposal.
   596	
   597	
   598	From the key exchange based on the DP, and analogously to the ElGamal cryptosystem, we can derive a public key cryptosystem whose cryptanalysis is computationally equivalent to the DHDP.
   599	We will give it in a general setting for semigroups, but let us introduce first the following notation.
   600	Let $t \in \mathbb{Z}^{+}$ and consider a one-to-one map $\beta : G \longrightarrow \mathbb{Z}_{2}^{t}$; so, for $x \in G$, $\beta(x)$ is the binary representation of $t$ digits of $x$.
   601	Moreover, we denote by $\oplus$ the bitwise \textsf{xor} operation in $\mathbb{Z}_{2}^{t}$.
   602	
   603	
   604	\begin{prot}[ElGamal DP protocol (EGDP protocol)] \label{prot2}
   605	Let $G$ be a semigroup and assume that $A, B \subseteq G$ are two subsemigroups such that $a b = b a$ for every $a \in A$ and $b \in B$.
   606	Let $m$ be the message that Bob desires to send Alice.
   607	\begin{enumerate}
   608	\item \label{prot2.1}
   609	Alice chooses $x \in G$ such that $x a \neq a x$, for every $a \in A$, and chooses $a_{1}, a_{2} \in A$.
   610	\item \label{prot2.2}
   611	Alice makes public the pair $(x, a_{1} x a_{2})$.
   612	\item \label{prot2.3}
   613	Bob chooses $b_{1}, b_{2} \in B$ randomly and such that $x b_{i} \neq b_{i} x$ for $i = 1,2$ and sends Alice the pair $(f,d) = (b_{1} x b_{2}, \beta^{-1}(\beta(m) \oplus \beta(b_{1} a_{1} x a_{2} b_{2}))$.
   614	\item \label{prot2.4}
   615	Alice recovers $m$ by computing $m = \beta^{-1}(\beta(d) \oplus \beta(a_{1} f a_{2}))$.
   616	\end{enumerate}
   617	\end{prot}
   618	
   619	
   620	Part \ref{prot2.4} of the above protocol is correct because
   621	\begin{align*}
   622	  \beta(d) \oplus \beta(a_{1} f a_{2})
   623	    & = \beta(d) \oplus \beta(a_{1} b_{1} x b_{2} a_{2}) \\
   624	    & = \beta(\beta^{-1}(\beta(m) \oplus \beta(b_{1} a_{1} x a_{2} b_{2}))) \oplus \beta(a_{1} b_{1} x b_{2} a_{2}) \\
   625	    & = \beta(m) \oplus \beta(b_{1} a_{1} x a_{2} b_{2}) \oplus \beta(a_{1} b_{1} x b_{2} a_{2}) \\
   626	    & = \beta(m)
   627	\end{align*}
   628	since $\beta(b_{1} a_{1} x a_{2} b_{2}) = \beta(a_{1} b_{1} x b_{2} a_{2})$ as $b_{i} a_{i} = a_{i} b_{i}$ for $i = 1,2$.
   629	Consequently,
   630	\[
   631	  \beta^{-1}(\beta(d) \oplus \beta(a_{1} f a_{2})) = \beta^{-1}(\beta(m)) = m.
   632	\]
   633	
   634	
   635	We point out that if we are considering a ring $R$ instead of a semigroup $G$, then we can substitute the map $\beta : G \longrightarrow \mathbb{Z}_{2}^{t}$ by the identity map in $R$ and the \textsf{xor} operation $\oplus$ by the addition in $R$.
   636	Here we need the existence of a zero element in order to get $m = d - a_{1} f a_{2}$.
   637	
   638	
   639	\begin{expl} \label{expl1}
   640	\begin{enumerate}
   641	\item \label{expl1.1}
   642	If the semigroup $G$ is commutative, then we have that EGDP corresponds to the ElGamal public key cryptosystem defined by the action of $G$ over itself (cf.\ \cite{Maze2007}).
   643	In this case, as we pointed out in Section~\ref{Intro}, the security of the cryptosystem is based on the difficulty of what the authors in \cite{Maze2007} called the SAP.
   644	
   645	\item \label{expl1.2}
   646	Let us consider the ring $E_{p}^{(m)}$.
   647	Then Alice and Bob can agree in a public element $M \in E_{p}^{(m)}$.
   648	Now let $S \in E_{p}^{(m)}$ be the secret that Bob wants to send Alice.
   649	Then $S$ may be sent in a confidential manner in the following way:
   650	\begin{enumerate}
   651	\item Alice chooses $N \in E_{p}^{(m)}$ such that $N M \neq M N$ and two elements $A_{1}, A_{2} \in H(M)$ (see equation (\ref{eq6})), and publishes her public key $(N, A_{1} N A_{2})$.
   652	\item Bob chooses randomly two other elements $B_{1}, B_{2} \in \mathrm{Cen}(M)$ (or simply $B_{1}, B_{2} \in H(M)$ as Alice) and sends her the pair given by $(F,D) = (B_{1} N B_{2}, S + B_{1} A_{1} N A_{2} B_{2})$.
   653	\item Alice recovers $S$ by computing $D - A_{1} F A_{2}$ due to the fact that $A_{i}$ and $B_{i}$ commute for $i = 1,2$.
   654	\end{enumerate}
   655	\end{enumerate}
   656	\end{expl}
   657	
   658	
   659	The following result shows the relation between the cryptanalysis of the EGDP and DHDP protocols.
   660	
   661	\begin{thm}
   662	Breaking the EGDP protocol is equivalent to solve the DHDP.
   663	\end{thm}
   664	\begin{proof}
   665	Assume that Eve can solve the DHDP and she wants to get $m$ from
   666	\[
   667	  (b_{1} x b_{2}, \beta^{-1}(\beta(m) \oplus \beta(b_{1} a_{1} x a_{2} b_{2}))).
   668	\]
   669	Since Eve is able to solve the DHDP, then she gets $b_{1} a_{1} x a_{2} b_{2}$ from $b_{1} x b_{2}$ and the Alice's public key $a_{1} x a_{2}$.
   670	Thus she can recover
   671	\[
   672	  m
   673	  =
   674	  \beta^{-1}(\beta(m) \oplus \beta(b_{1} a_{1} x a_{2} b_{2}) \oplus \beta(b_{1} a_{1} x a_{2} b_{2})).
   675	\]
   676	
   677	Now assume that Eve can solve the EGDP.
   678	Then she can obtain any message $m$ from the information $x, a_{1} x a_{2}, b_{1} x b_{2}, \beta^{-1} (\beta(m) \oplus \beta(b_{1} a_{1} x a_{2} b_{2}))$.
   679	If Eve wishes to get $b_{1} a_{1} x a_{2} b_{2}$ from $x, a_{1} x a_{2}, b_{1} x b_{2}$, then she encrypts $m$ using $b_{1} x b_{2}$ as random element in step~\ref{prot2.3} of Protocol~\ref{prot2}, to get $d = \beta^{-1}(\beta(m) \oplus \beta(b_{1} a_{1} x a_{2} b_{2}))$ and thus, she gets the solution to the DHDP.
   680	\end{proof}

