   680	\end{proof}
   681	
   682	
   683	Let us show how an analogous reasoning to the one used in the cryptanalysis introduced in \cite{Kamal2012} may apply to break the EGDP protocol.
   684	Let us assume that Bob sends Alice the pair
   685	\[
   686	  (f,d)
   687	  =
   688	  (b_{1} x b_{2}, \beta^{-1}(\beta(m) \oplus \beta(b_{1} a_{1} x a_{2} b_{2})))
   689	\]
   690	as in the EGDP algorithm and that Eve is able to find $w_{1}, w_{2}$ commuting with every element in $A$ and such that $f w_{2} = w_{1} x$.
   691	Suppose also that $w_{2}$ is invertible in the semigroup $G$.
   692	Then
   693	\[
   694	  w_{1} a_{1} x a_{2} w_{2}^{-1}
   695	  =
   696	  a_{1} w_{1} x w_{2}^{-1} a_{2}
   697	  =
   698	  a_{1} f a_{2}
   699	\]
   700	and thus $\beta^{-1}(\beta(d) \oplus \beta(w_{1} a_{1} x a_{2} w_{2}^{-1})) = m$.
   701	
   702	
   703	Therefore, the further the subgroup of units in $G$ is from being $G$, the more difficult will be to apply this reasoning to break the EGDP protocol.
   704	This applies to the case of $E_{p}^{(m)}$ since as we mentioned previously, a suitable choice of $p$ and $m$ provides a ring such that almost all its elements are not units.
   705	
   706	
   707	We will finish this paper by giving some conditions on the public key used for the EGDP protocol in the case of the ring $E_{p}^{(m)}$ in order to avoid an attack by reducing it to find a solution to a certain SAP.
   708	
   709	
   710	Let us assume that Alice's public key is given by the pair $(X, A_{1} X A_{2})$ and let Eve be an attacker.
   711	If Eve is able to find $M \in Z(E_{p}^{(m)})$ such that it is a solution of the SAP $M X = A_{1} X A_{2}$, then Eve may choose $H \in Z(E_{p}^{(m)})$ and invertible, say
   712	\[
   713	  H
   714	  =
   715	%  \mathrm{diag}
   716	%  \left(
   717	%    h_{0},
   718	%    h_{0} + p h_{1},
   719	%    \ldots,
   720	%    h_{0} + p h_{1} + \cdots + p^{m-1} h_{m-1}
   721	%  \right),
   722	  \mathrm{diag}
   723	  \left(
   724	    h_{0},
   725	    h_{0} + h_{1} p,
   726	    \ldots,
   727	    \sum_{l=0}^{m-2} h_{l} p^{l},
   728	    \sum_{l=0}^{m-1} h_{l} p^{l}
   729	  \right),
   730	\]
   731	with $h_{0} \not \equiv_{p} 0$ (cf.\ \cite{Climent2014d}).
   732	Thus Eve writes $M H^{-1} H$ and gets that
   733	\[
   734	  A_{1} X A_{2}
   735	  =
   736	  M X
   737	  =
   738	  M H^{-1} X H,
   739	\]
   740	solving the DP and therefore she solves the DHDP.
   741	
   742	
   743	As previously noted, the next result gives conditions on the public key of the EGDP protocol that avoid breaking it by reducing the DP to a SAP as above explained.
   744	Its proof is a direct application of Corollary~\ref{conditionSAP}.
   745	
   746	
   747	\begin{cor}
   748	In the precedent situation, let $(X,P)$, with $P = A_{1} X A_{2}$, be the public key of some user of the EGDP protocol over the ring $E_{p}^{(m)}$.
   749	If there exists $k = 1,2,\ldots,m$ such that $X_{i,k} \not\equiv_{p} 0$ for every $i = 1,2,\ldots,m$ and there exists $j$ such that $X_{j,k}^{-1} P_{j,k} \not \equiv_{p} X_{1,k}^{-1} P_{1,k}$, then an attacker cannot break the EGDP by reducing it to solve a SAP over the ring $E_{p}^{(m)}$.
   750	\end{cor}
